{
  "id": "a2-rational-05",
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Series and sigma notation",
  "xp": 18,
  "tags": [
    "algebra",
    "sequences",
    "series"
  ],
  "learn": [
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "Adding up a sequence",
      "body": "A series is the sum of the terms of a sequence. Sigma notation writes it compactly: $\\sum_{k=1}^{5}(2k+1)$ means plug in $k=1,2,3,4,5$ and add: $3+5+7+9+11=35$."
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "kind": "example",
      "title": "Arithmetic series",
      "body": "The sum of the first $n$ terms is $S_n=\\frac{n(a_1+a_n)}{2}$.",
      "steps": [
        "Add $1+2+\\cdots+100$: pair first and last",
        "$S_{100}=\\frac{100(1+100)}{2}=5050$",
        "For $5,8,\\ldots$ ten terms: $a_{10}=32$",
        "$S_{10}=\\frac{10(5+32)}{2}=185$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "kind": "example",
      "title": "Geometric series",
      "body": "The sum of the first $n$ terms is $S_n=\\frac{a_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$.",
      "steps": [
        "For $2,6,18,54,162$: $a_1=2$, $r=3$, $n=5$",
        "$S_5=\\frac{2(1-3^5)}{1-3}$",
        "$=\\frac{2(-242)}{-2}=242$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p4",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "Infinite geometric series",
      "body": "If $|r|<1$ the terms shrink and the total settles to $\\frac{a_1}{1-r}$. For $6+3+1.5+\\cdots$ this is $\\frac{6}{1-0.5}=12$. If $|r|\\ge 1$ the sum grows without limit."
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "type": "type_answer",
      "prompt": "$1+2+3+\\cdots+10=$ ?",
      "accepted": [
